ortray him as a foundational figure in the logic of thought, developing a theory of individuation and expression and exploring internal differences and differential relations among particulars. He is depicted as presenting ideas as virtual multiplicities—composed of differential relations and singular points—a view that informs discussions of how ideas form a connected field, and which is explicitly linked to Bergson through the notion of Leibnizian possibles in potential recollections.\n\nIn metaphysics and epistemology, Leibniz is associated with the law of continuity, the principle of the identity of indiscernibles, and the notion that the world can be understood in terms of monads—simple, immaterial substances whose relations generate the apparent complexity of reality. He is described as expressing the world through monads and as the author of letters to Arnauld, contributing to debates about the grounding of finite determination and the infinite movement of evanescence. His correspondences and ideas about essential vs. inessential, genus and species, and the movement between these distinctions are noted as part of a broader program to go beyond a purely organic account of reality, while still engaging with questions of ground and representation, especially in contrast to Hegel.\n\nMathematically, Leibniz is credited with showing that calculus is the instrument of a combinatory, enabling the expression of transcendent problems that could not be posed or solved before his work. He is described as employing methods of differentiation and reciprocal determination, emphasizing differential relations and singularities as central to the formation of ideas. His infinitesimal analysis and attention to the infinitely small are highlighted as crucial to understanding change and continuity, including discussions of time, repetition, and the relation between past and present.\n\nThe descriptions also present Leibniz as a model of rule-based reasoning—symbolized by the “Leibnizian game of chess”—and as a thinker who integrates not only technical mathematical insights but a robust philosophical framework: ideas are dynamically connected, continuously reinterpreted through differential relations, and grounded in a coherent metaphysical system. Across these strands, Leibniz is positioned as a pivotal figure who influenced later debates on memory, potentiality, and the structure of thought, while simultaneously being contrasted with Hegel in terms of scope and development.", "ALBERT EINSTEIN:Albert Einstein is a renowned theoretical physicist referenced in this document. He is best known for formulating the theory of relativity, a framework in which time and space are interwoven and relative rather than absolute. Einstein's insights introduced key concepts such as time dilation, illustrating that the passage of time can vary for observers in different frames of reference and under different gravitational fields, thereby challenging the classical notion of a universal, absolute temporal flow. His theoretical contributions have profoundly impacted physics, redefining our understanding of space, time, and motion.", "MARCEL PROUST:Marcel Proust, French novelist (1871-1922), author of In Search of Lost Time (À la recherche du temps perdu); celebrated for memory, time, and social analysis, and regarded as a leading figure in modern literature.)<|COMPLETE|>", "G.B.R. RIEMANN:Referenced figure in Bergsonism; cited with pages 39-40 and 79", "GEORGES BATTAILLE:Georges Bataille is a French writer and philosopher cited in the text, associated with discussions of sumptuary and nonproductive expenditures, the energy of nature, and the concept of desiring-machines and the production of production.)(\"entity\"", "ANTONIN ARTAUD:Antonin Artaud is a French dramatist, playwright, poet, actor, and theatre theorist. The text references him as the author of a quotation from 1948 that is used to illustrate ideas about the body without organs (BwO) and desiring-production. It also notes that Artaud discovered the body without organs when he found himself with no shape or form, highlighting his engagement with radical, transformative conceptions of the body in relation to performance. Through his work in poetry, drama, and theory, Artaud is presented as a central figure whose writings and practice challenge conventional theatrical forms by foregrounding corporeal intensity and the destabilization of ordinary bodily and perceptual organization within the theatre.", "JUDGE SCHREBER:Judge Schreber was a German judge and the author of Memoirs of My Nervous Illness. In the text, he is cited as a case in psychoanalytic discussion—a historical figure whose illness is analyzed within psychoanalytic discourse.", "VICTOR TAUSK:Psychoanalyst who discussed the paranoiac machine and projection in relation to desiring-machines; cited in this text)<|COMPLETE|>No additional entities of type organization, geo, or event were found in the provided text beyond the already identified individuals (Antonin Artaud, Judge Schreber, Victor Tausk).<|COMPLETE|>", "ROBERT GIE:Robert Gie is described as the designer of paranoiac electrical machines who allegedly joined forces with tormenting currents", "SIGMUND FREUD:SIGMUND FREUD is presented as the Austrian neurologist who founded psychoanalysis and stands as a central figure in shaping modern understandings of the mind. The descriptions collectively emphasize his foundational concepts, including the pleasure principle, the Id, the development of the ego, habit, and the significance of repetition in psychic life. They note his exploration of psychic repression and the controversial role of incest within psychosexual development, as well as the notion of transitional points or turning points within Freudian theory. The material highlights Freud’s influence on clinical interpretation, referencing his analysis of Schreber’s illness and the pivotal interpretive shifts that accompany psychoanalytic readings of pathological states. Additionally, the descriptions address his metapsychological distinctions, notably the death instinct (Thanatos) and its differentiation from Eros, signaling a dual-drive framework within his theory. Overall, the synthesis portrays Freud as the founder of psychoanalysis whose work spans structural models of the psyche (the Id and ego), drives and motivations (pleasure principle, repetition, habit), developmental insights (including psychosexual dynamics like incest), clinical interpretation of neurotic states, and the crucial life–death instinct dichotomy.", "HOLDERLIN:German poet; cited in the discussion as part of a historical span from Holderlin to Nietzsche, illustrating intellectual lineage", "G. DE CLERAMBAULT:French psychiatrist; associated with the concept of delirium and automatism, referenced in the discussion of materialist psychiatry", "MONAKOW:A neurologist mentioned in the discussion of desiring-machines and their breakdowns, cited as part of Monakow and Mourgue's study on fragmentation and the introduction of desire into neurology", "MOURGUE:A co-researcher with Monakow in the study of desiring-machines, associated with the ideas of detachment, fragmentation, and the machine-like processes in neurology", "LACAN:LACAN refers to Jacques Lacan, a prominent French psychoanalyst whose work is described through core Lacanian concepts such as repetition, the imaginary, and the role of the phallus in relation to the subject. The descriptions portray Lacan as discovering the symbolic “phallus” behind virtual or partial objects, thereby expanding the conceptual scope of psychoanalytic theory to encompass repetition and desire. He is invoked to postulate and describe the interplay of elements within desiring-production, emphasizing machinic over etymological relations. In this view, the symbolic phallus mediates subjectivity and desire, while the dynamics of the imaginary and repetition structure the subject’s relation to desire. Together, these descriptions position Jacques Lacan as a central figure in modern psychoanalytic theory, integrating structural, linguistic, and functional dimensions of desire and shifting focus from purely historical or etymological origins to the machinic organization of desiring-production."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_243ca80db91825f7b2bcc09b409896e5785803fc848fe2622c1473207127cac5_v2"}